  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For an array BBB of length NNN, we define f(B)f(B)f(B) as follows.\n\nConsider the following process:\n\n\nInitially, you have a **bag** containing the elements B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\dots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​.\n\nA bag treated as a multiset, meaning it may contain duplicate values. Your score is initially 000.\nN−1N-1N−1 times, perform the following operation:\n\nChoose and remove two elements XXX and YYY from the bag.\nAdd max⁡(X,Y)\\max(X,Y)max(X,Y) to your score.\nInsert Z=X&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;YZ = X\\ \\&amp;\\ YZ=X&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Y back into the bag.\nIn particular, the size of the bag will decrease by exactly 111 after each operation.\n\n\nf(B)f(B)f(B) is defined to be the **maximum** possible final score you can attain as a result of this process.\n\nYou are given two integers NNN and MMM.\n\nYour task is to compute the sum of f(B)f(B)f(B) across all integer arrays of length NNN whose elements lie in [1,M][1, M][1,M].\n\nThat is, compute\n\n∑B∈[1,M]Nf(B)\\sum_{B \\in [1,M]^N} f(B)B∈[1,M]N∑​f(B)Since the answer can be large, output it modulo 109+710^9+7109+7.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains an integer TTT denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two space-separated integers NNN and MMM.\n\n\nOutput Format\n\nFor each test case, print a single integer — the value of ∑B∈[1,M]Nf(B)(mod109+7)\\sum_{B \\in [1,M]^N} f(B) \\pmod{10^9+7}∑B∈[1,M]N​f(B)(mod109+7).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1041 \\le T \\le 10^41≤T≤104",
      "1≤N≤2⋅1051 \\le N \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤M≤2⋅1051 \\le M \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤M≤2⋅105",
      "The sum of MMM over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3 6\n2 2\n1 500",
        "output": "1827\n7\n0",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: We showcase one optimal sequence of operations for the initial array B=[4,5,6]B = [4, 5, 6]B=[4,5,6].\nInitial bag: {4,5,6}\\{4,5,6\\}{4,5,6}, score =0=0=0\nWe perform N−1=2N-1 = 2N−1=2 operations.\nOperation 1: choose X=4X=4X=4, Y=6Y=6Y=6\n\nRemove 444 and 666 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ bag becomes {5}\\{5\\}{5}\nAdd max⁡(4,6)=6\\max(4,6)=6max(4,6)=6 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ score =6=6=6\nInsert (4&6)=4(4 \\& 6)=4(4&6)=4 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ bag becomes {5,4}\\{5,4\\}{5,4}\n\nOperation 2: choose X=4X=4X=4, Y=5Y=5Y=5\n\nRemove 444 and 555 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ bag becomes {}\\{\\}{}\nAdd max⁡(4,5)=5\\max(4,5)=5max(4,5)=5 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ score =11=11=11\nInsert (4&5)=4(4 \\& 5)=4(4&5)=4 ⇒\\Rightarrow⇒ bag becomes {4}\\{4\\}{4}\n\nFinal score =11=11=11.\nThere are MN=63=216M^N = 6^3 = 216MN=63=216 possible initial arrays.\nIt can be verified that the sum of scores across them all equals 182718271827.\nTest case 222: We showcase one optimal sequence of operations for the initial array B=[1,2]B = [1, 2]B=[1,2].\nInitial bag: {1,2}\\{1,2\\}{1,2}, score =0=0=0\nWe can only perform N−1=1N-1 = 1N−1=1 operation, which adds max⁡(1,2)=2\\max(1, 2) = 2max(1,2)=2 to the score and inserts 1 & 2=01\\ \\&\\ 2 = 01 & 2=0 back into the bag.\nSo, the final score is forced to be 222.\nIt can be seen that the arrays [1,2],[2,1],[2,2][1, 2], [2, 1], [2, 2][1,2],[2,1],[2,2] all have a final score of 222, while [1,1][1, 1][1,1] has a score of 111.\nSumming all these up gives 2+2+2+1=72+2+2+1 = 72+2+2+1=7."
      }
    ]
